## Step 2 — implement
This is the highest-stakes change in the item: it alters migration behavior for every
`.beads` store on this machine, and 17 are live. Two rules follow from that.

- Order matters. Fix B (rebuild copies columns discovered from `pragma_table_info`) must
  land together with fix C (migration ledger), B first, because B is what makes C's single
  final backfill pass lossless.
- The backfill is exactly "run everything once more, then record everything". Behaviour-
  identical to today for one open, so it cannot produce a state today's code would not
  already produce. Any attempt to DETECT which migrations already ran and skip some can
  leave a store half-migrated. The bead says do not; do not.

The regression test is not optional garnish — it is the thing that stops this recurring.
Enumerate columns from `pragma_table_info`, never from a hand-written list: a hand-written
list would have been written in the same breath as 022's and would have carried the same
three omissions.

Hard boundaries: run migrations only against databases under your own `mktemp -d`. Never
touch `/home/ben/projects/*/.beads/`, any other worktree's `.beads/`, or
`~/.local/bin/bd`.

Prove your fix with a real reproduction, not only unit tests — the unit suite passed
throughout while this bug was live, because it never opens a populated store twice. In a
temp store with a binary you build: write a value into `pinned` and `claim_expires_at`,
run a second `bd` process (`bd --no-daemon list` is enough), and read the values back.
Paste that transcript into your build report.

